  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1046 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nUpper ridge building in nicely over the Desert Southwest this\nmorning, ahead of an advancing upper trough over the Gulf of\nAlaska and west coast of the US. Strong sub-tropical jet persists\nover the southern Baja and central Mexico. Dry with cool\ntemperatures continuing today and Wednesday under the upper ridge.\nSouthern Dona Ana County will be close to freezing tonight but\ndecided to leave out of a warning at this time. \n\nThursday and Friday...west coast upper trough continues to\ndecelerate as it moves inland due to sluggish upper low over the\ncentral US/Great Lakes. Trough reaches Arizona Thursday and closes\noff a low over the southern Baja. The low is able to tap into some\nof the sub-tropical moisture as the main sub-tropical jet moves\noff to the east. This moisture is able to make it as far north as\nnorthern Mexico before the northerly winds spilling through the\nridge north of the low end any hopes of moisture getting up to\nour area. Thus other than a few high clouds, this system looks to\nhave little impact on the CWA. \n\nSaturday into next week...upper ridge behind the exiting low will\ndominate Saturday and Sunday...clearing any clouds from the sky\nand allowing temperatures to warm back above normal. Next Pacific\ntrough sweeps through well to our north Sunday night. Little or\nno chance for any precipitation. Could be a wind maker but right\nnow the timing of the trough coming through Sunday night is not\nconducive to strong winds. Both Sunday and Monday on the breezy\nside but no impactful winds. A change of the trough timing could\nchange this though.",
